\title{Sheaf-Theoretic Data Integration: Exact Laws, Threshold Parameters, and the Betti Number of the Overlap Nerve}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 16, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a complete sheaf-theoretic account of databases with missing entries. A table with $n$ columns and $k$ rows is regarded as a family of local sections of a sheaf on the column set, each row being a section over its observed support; imputation becomes gluing. We prove that gluing is equivalent to pairwise agreement on overlaps, that the space of completions of a gluable table has exactly $q^{u}$ elements where $u$ is the number of wholly unobserved columns and $q$ the alphabet size, and that column-mean imputation coincides identically with sheaf imputation on gluable real-valued data. For the model in which cells are independently missing with rate $r$ and observed cells are independent uniform, we compute the probability of the sheaf condition in closed form, $P(\mathrm{sheaf}) = (qA^{k} - (q-1)r^{k})^{n}$ with $A = r + (1-r)/q$; the law is exponential in the number of columns, \emph{increasing} in the missing rate, and admits no representation $(1-r)^{C}$. We compute the first two moments of the number $N$ of global sections and derive matching bounds $\E[N]^{2}/\E[N^{2}] \le P \le \E[N]$, tight at $r \in \{0,1\}$ and strict inside. Writing $\tail(k,r) = \Pr[\Bin(k,1-r)\ge 2]$, we prove the weighted-tail identity and the two-sided sandwich $(1-1/q)\tail \le 1-\beta \le \tail$, whence a genuine threshold at $n\cdot\tail(k,r)\asymp 1$. Finally, the data sheaf is flasque, hence acyclic ($H^{0}\cong\bbk^{n}$, $H^{1}=0$ for every cover), so no cohomological invariant measures imputation difficulty for raw records; but for the calibration sheaf of inter-source offsets we prove $\dim H^{1} + \#\text{sources} = \#\text{overlaps} + \#\text{components}$, i.e.\ $\dim H^{1}$ is the first Betti number of the overlap nerve, with $H^{1}=0$ iff the nerve is a forest, and $\dim H^{1}\le b_{1}$ once triple overlaps are admitted.
\end{abstract}

