\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
Missing data is usually treated as an estimation problem: a blank cell is an unknown number, and imputation is the business of guessing it. This paper takes seriously an alternative framing suggested by geometry. A table with holes is a family of \emph{partial} observations, each defined on its own subset of features, and the question of whether the holes can be filled consistently is the question of whether these partial observations \emph{glue}.

Gluing is the defining property of a sheaf: a family of local sections agreeing on all pairwise overlaps determines a global section. Reading a database this way---the column set as base space, the observed support of each row as an open set, the row as a local section---raises three questions.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)] \textbf{Combinatorics.} When exactly does a table glue, and how many completions does it have?
\item[(2)] \textbf{Probability.} For a randomly masked table, what is the probability of gluability, and what single parameter controls it?
\item[(3)] \textbf{Topology.} Is there a cohomological obstruction to imputation, and what does it measure?
\end{itemize}
We answer all three. For raw records the answers to (1) and (3) are deflationary: gluing is exactly pairwise consistency, and the relevant cohomology vanishes identically. The answer to (2) is an exact law with a clean threshold. Pursuing (3) past its negative answer produces the paper's most substantial theorem: the obstruction to \emph{multi-source calibration} is the first Betti number of the overlap nerve.

\subsection{Prior expectations and their fate}
Three natural conjectures motivated this work.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(C1)] The probability that a random table with missing rate $r$ glues is $(1-r)^{C}$, with $C$ counting overlapping constraints.
\item[(C2)] Sheaf imputation---filling blanks by finding the nearest global section---outperforms mean and nearest-neighbour imputation for $r < 1/2$ and large feature count.
\item[(C3)] Data imputation is a sheaf cohomology problem.
\end{itemize}
Our findings: \textbf{(C1) is false} in functional form, in the location of its combinatorial factor, and in the \emph{direction} of its monotonicity; it is replaced by Theorem~\ref{thm:exact} and the threshold of Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}. \textbf{(C2) is false} for the constant data sheaf, because mean imputation and sheaf imputation are the same function on gluable data (Theorem~\ref{thm:meanimp}). \textbf{(C3) is true only in degree zero} for raw records (Theorem~\ref{thm:acyclic}), and becomes a genuine $H^{1}$ statement only for calibration coefficients (Theorem~\ref{thm:betti}).

\section{The model}

\begin{definition}[Partial database]
Fix $n,k\ge 0$ and an alphabet $\Sigma$ with $|\Sigma| = q \ge 1$. A \emph{partial database} is a map $D : \{1,\dots,k\}\times\{1,\dots,n\}\to \Sigma\cup\{\bot\}$, where $\bot$ denotes a missing entry. The \emph{observed support} of row $j$ is $U_{j} = \{c : D(j,c)\neq\bot\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Data sheaf]
The \emph{data sheaf} $\mathcal{F}$ on the column set assigns to a subset $V$ the set $\mathcal{F}(V) = \Sigma^{V}$, with restriction given by forgetting columns. Row $j$ is a section $D_{j}\in\mathcal{F}(U_{j})$, so $D$ is a family of local sections over the cover $\{U_{1},\dots,U_{k}\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Section, gluability, pairwise consistency]
A \emph{global section} of $D$ is a complete record $g:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\Sigma$ with $g(c) = D(j,c)$ whenever $D(j,c)\neq\bot$; $D$ is \emph{gluable} if one exists, and $\Sec(D)$ denotes the set of global sections. $D$ is \emph{pairwise consistent} if $D(j,c)\neq\bot$ and $D(j',c)\neq\bot$ imply $D(j,c)=D(j',c)$; equivalently, $D_{j}$ and $D_{j'}$ agree on $U_{j}\cap U_{j'}$.
\end{definition}

Write $\Obs(D) = \{c : \exists j,\ D(j,c)\neq\bot\}$ and $u(D) = n - |\Obs(D)|$.

\begin{definition}[MCAR uniform model]
Each of the $nk$ cells is independently missing with probability $r\in[0,1]$; each observed cell independently carries a uniform value in $\Sigma$. Write $P_{n,k,q}(r)$ for the probability of gluability and $N$ for the number of global sections.
\end{definition}

\section{Gluing, counting, and imputation}

\begin{theorem}[Gluing $=$ pairwise consistency]\label{thm:glue}
For $q\ge 1$, a partial database $D$ is gluable if and only if it is pairwise consistent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) If $g$ is a section and $D(j,c)=v$, $D(j',c)=v'$, then $v=g(c)=v'$. ($\Leftarrow$) Define $g(c)$ to be the common observed value in column $c$ if any row observes $c$, and an arbitrary alphabet element otherwise; pairwise consistency makes this well defined.
\end{proof}

There is no higher obstruction: agreement on pairwise overlaps suffices, with no condition on triples. For a column $c$ put $\Val(D,c) = \{v\in\Sigma : v = D(j,c) \text{ for all } j \text{ with } D(j,c)\neq\bot\}$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:product}
$D$ is gluable iff $\Val(D,c)\neq\varnothing$ for every $c$, and $\Sec(D) = \prod_{c}\Val(D,c)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Section count]\label{thm:count}
If $D$ is pairwise consistent then $|\Sec(D)| = q^{\,u(D)}$, where $u(D)$ is the number of columns observed by no row.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Proposition~\ref{prop:product} the count is $\prod_{c}|\Val(D,c)|$. Unobserved columns give $|\Val| = q$; observed columns give $|\Val| = 1$ by pairwise consistency. Splitting the product over $\Obs(D)$ and its complement gives $q^{u(D)}$.
\end{proof}

The sheaf-theoretic degrees of freedom sit precisely at the wholly unobserved columns: a column observed once is as constrained as a column observed a thousand times.

\begin{proposition}[Masking never breaks gluing]\label{prop:mask}
Let $g:\{1,\dots,n\}\to\Sigma$ and let $M$ be an arbitrary mask; set $D(j,c) = g(c)$ for $(j,c)\in M$ and $\bot$ otherwise. Then $D$ is gluable, with $g$ a global section. If moreover every column is observed by at least one row, then $\Sec(D) = \{g\}$: sheaf imputation recovers the ground truth exactly.
\end{proposition}

This already refutes (C1) at the deterministic level: if data are generated by masking a ground truth, the sheaf condition holds with probability one for every missing rate, so no law $(1-r)^{C}$ with $C>0$ can hold. The independent-uniform model is precisely the model in which the sheaf condition is nontrivial.

Let now entries be real, $D(j,c)\in\mathbb{R}\cup\{\bot\}$, and let $\widehat{\mu}(c) = |O_{c}|^{-1}\sum_{j\in O_{c}}D(j,c)$ with $O_{c} = \{j : D(j,c)\neq\bot\}$.

\begin{theorem}[Mean imputation $=$ sheaf imputation]\label{thm:meanimp}
If $D$ is pairwise consistent and $D(j_{0},c) = v$ for some row $j_{0}$, then $\widehat{\mu}(c) = v$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pairwise consistency makes every observed entry of column $c$ equal to $v$, so the sum is $|O_{c}|\,v$.
\end{proof}

This refutes (C2) for the constant data sheaf in the strongest possible way: not ``mean imputation is competitive'' but ``mean imputation \emph{is} sheaf imputation''. Any strict superiority claim must come from a sheaf with nontrivial restriction maps.

\section{The exact probability law}

\begin{lemma}[Agreement probability]\label{lem:agree}
If exactly $j$ rows observe a fixed column, the probability that they all carry the same value is $q^{\,1-j}$ for $j\ge 1$, and $1$ for $j=0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The number of $v\in\Sigma^{k}$ constant on a set of size $j\ge 1$ is $q^{k-j+1}$; divide by $q^{k}$. Equivalently, $\#\{v : v \text{ constant on } S\}\cdot q^{|S|-1} = q^{k}$.
\end{proof}

For a row set $S$ let $w(S) = (1-r)^{|S|}r^{\,k-|S|}$ be the probability that exactly the rows in $S$ observe a given column, and let $\beta_{\Sigma}(k,q,r) = \sum_{S}w(S)q^{\,1-|S|}$ (with the exponent truncated at $0$ for $S = \varnothing$) be the per-column consistency probability.

\begin{theorem}[Exact law]\label{thm:exact}
For $q\ge 1$ and $r\in[0,1]$, put $A = r + \frac{1-r}{q}$ and $\beta(k,q,r) = qA^{k} - (q-1)r^{k}$. Then $\beta_{\Sigma} = \beta$ and
\[
P_{n,k,q}(r) = \beta(k,q,r)^{\,n}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\emph{Factorisation.} Expanding the product over columns of the per-column sums yields the sum over all mask configurations of $\prod_{c}w(M(c))q^{1-|M(c)|}$, which is exactly $P_{n,k,q}(r)$; hence $P=\beta_{\Sigma}^{n}$. \emph{Closed form.} Using $\sum_{S\subseteq[k]}a^{|S|}b^{k-|S|} = (a+b)^{k}$ with $a = (1-r)/q$, $b=r$, the sum $\sum_{S}w(S)q^{-|S|+1}$ evaluates to $q(r+\frac{1-r}{q})^{k}$; this over-counts the empty pattern, whose true weight is $r^{k}$ rather than $q\,r^{k}$, and correcting that single term subtracts $(q-1)r^{k}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The exponent is $n$, the number of columns; no binomial coefficient occurs in the formula.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity in the missing rate]\label{thm:mono}
For $q\ge 1$ and $0\le r_{1}\le r_{2}\le 1$ one has $\beta(k,q,r_{1})\le\beta(k,q,r_{2})$; hence $P_{n,k,q}(r)$ is increasing in $r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $A_{i} = r_{i} + (1-r_{i})/q$, a telescoping computation gives
\[
\beta(k,q,r_{2})-\beta(k,q,r_{1}) = (q-1)(r_{2}-r_{1})\Big(\sum_{i<k}A_{2}^{i}A_{1}^{k-1-i} - \sum_{i<k}r_{2}^{i}r_{1}^{k-1-i}\Big),
\]
and $0\le r_{i}\le A_{i}$ makes the bracket nonnegative.
\end{proof}

A missing cell \emph{deletes} a consistency constraint; more missingness means more gluability. The conjectured law has the monotonicity reversed.

\begin{theorem}[No missing-rate power law]
For $n\ge 1$, $k\ge 2$, $q\ge 2$ there is no exponent $C$ with $P_{n,k,q}(r) = (1-r)^{C}$ for all $r\in[0,1]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At $r=0$ we have $\beta = q^{1-k}<1$, so $P = q^{n(1-k)}<1$, whereas $(1-r)^{C}=1$ there for every $C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
For $k\ge 2$, $q\ge 2$, $r<1$ we have $\beta(k,q,r)<1$, so $P_{n,k,q}(r)$ decays exponentially in $n$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Moments]\label{thm:moments}
Let $N$ be the number of global sections. Then
\[
\E[N] = (qA^{k})^{n}, \qquad \E[N^{2}] = \big(qA^{k} + (q^{2}-q)r^{k}\big)^{n}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A column with at least one observation admits at most one completion, so there $N$ is $\{0,1\}$-valued and $N^{2}=N$; only the empty pattern differs, contributing $q$ to $\E[N]$ and $q^{2}$ to $\E[N^{2}]$. Independence across columns gives the $n$-th powers.
\end{proof}

Write $u = qA^{k}$, $v = (q-1)r^{k}$, so $P = (u-v)^{n}$, $\E[N] = u^{n}$, $\E[N^{2}] = (u+qv)^{n}$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided moment bounds]\label{thm:pz}
For $q\ge 1$, $r\in[0,1]$,
\[
\frac{\E[N]^{2}}{\E[N^{2}]} \;\le\; P_{n,k,q}(r) \;\le\; \E[N].
\]
The lower bound is an equality at $r=0$ (for $k\ge1$) and at $r=1$, and is strict for $q\ge2$, $k\ge1$, $n\ge1$, $0<r<1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
It suffices to check the per-column inequality $u^{2}\le(u-v)(u+qv)$, i.e.\ $0\le q(q-1)^{2}r^{k}(A^{k}-r^{k})$, valid since $r\le A$. Equality holds exactly when $v=0$ ($r=0$) or $A^{k}=r^{k}$ ($r=1$ or $q=1$).
\end{proof}

\section{The difficulty parameter: a binomial tail}

\subsection{Where the binomial coefficient belongs}

\begin{theorem}[Pair union bound]\label{thm:pair}
For $q\ge1$, $r\in[0,1]$,
\[
1-\beta(k,q,r) \;\le\; \binom{k}{2}\Big(1-\frac1q\Big)(1-r)^{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $g(k) = 1-\beta(k,q,r)$ and $a = 1-1/q$, the increment is $g(k+1)-g(k) = (1-r)(q-1)(A^{k}-r^{k})$. The elementary bound $x^{k}-y^{k}\le k(x-y)$ for $0\le y\le x\le1$, applied with $x=A$, $y=r$, $A-r=(1-r)/q$, gives $g(k+1)-g(k)\le k\,a\,(1-r)^{2}$; summing the arithmetic progression yields $\binom{k}{2}a(1-r)^{2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two rows: exact]
For $k=2$ and $q\ge1$, $\beta(2,q,r) = 1-(1-\frac1q)(1-r)^{2}$, hence
$P_{n,2,q}(r) = \big(1-(1-\frac1q)(1-r)^{2}\big)^{n}$.
\end{theorem}

Thus the binomial coefficient of (C1) counts \emph{pairs of rows}, appears as a linear factor rather than an exponent, and multiplies $(1-r)^{2}$ rather than $(1-r)$.

\begin{corollary}
$P_{n,k,q}(r)\ge 1 - n\binom{k}{2}(1-\frac1q)(1-r)^{2}$, and, when the pair bound is at most $1$, also $P_{n,k,q}(r)\ge\big(1-\binom{k}{2}(1-\frac1q)(1-r)^{2}\big)^{n}$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The weighted tail identity and the sandwich}

\begin{definition}
With observation rate $p = 1-r$, set
\[
\tail(k,r) \;=\; \sum_{j=2}^{k}\binom{k}{j}p^{j}r^{\,k-j} \;=\; \Pr[\Bin(k,p)\ge 2] \;=\; 1 - r^{k} - k(1-r)r^{\,k-1}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Weighted tail identity]\label{thm:tailid}
For every $k$ and $q\ge1$,
\[
1-\beta(k,q,r) \;=\; \sum_{j=2}^{k}\binom{k}{j}(1-r)^{j}r^{\,k-j}\big(1-q^{\,1-j}\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\beta = \sum_{j=0}^{k}\binom{k}{j}p^{j}r^{k-j}q^{1-j} - (q-1)r^{k}$ and subtract from $1 = \sum_{j}\binom{k}{j}p^{j}r^{k-j}$ termwise. The $j=1$ term cancels identically because $q\cdot q^{-1}=1$, and the $j=0$ term $r^{k}(1-q)$ is cancelled exactly by the correction $+(q-1)r^{k}$. Only $j\ge2$ survives, with weights $1-q^{1-j}$.
\end{proof}

Only observation patterns with at least two observed rows can break the sheaf condition, and such a pattern breaks it with probability exactly $1-q^{1-j}$. Since $q^{1-j} = q\cdot q^{-j}\le 1/q$ for $j\ge2$, these weights lie in $[1-\frac1q,\,1]$.

\begin{theorem}[Tail sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
For $q\ge1$, $r\in[0,1]$,
\[
\Big(1-\frac1q\Big)\tail(k,r) \;\le\; 1-\beta(k,q,r) \;\le\; \tail(k,r),
\]
and consequently
\[
\big(1-\tail(k,r)\big)^{n} \;\le\; P_{n,k,q}(r) \;\le\; \Big(1-\big(1-\tfrac1q\big)\tail(k,r)\Big)^{n}.
\]
\end{theorem}

The two bounds differ only by the factor $1-1/q\ge\frac12$ for $q\ge2$: the binomial tail determines the per-column failure probability up to an absolute constant.

\begin{corollary}[Threshold]\label{cor:threshold}
\[
P_{n,k,q}(r)\le \exp\!\Big(-n\big(1-\tfrac1q\big)\tail(k,r)\Big), \qquad P_{n,k,q}(r)\ge 1 - n\,\tail(k,r).
\]
Hence $P\to1$ when $n\cdot\tail(k,r)\to0$ and $P\to0$ when $n\cdot\tail(k,r)\to\infty$: the difficulty parameter of a random database is $n\cdot\tail(k,r)$, with transition at $n\cdot\tail(k,r)\asymp1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:reconcile}
For $k\ge2$ and $r\in[0,1]$,
\[
\binom{k}{2}(1-r)^{2}r^{\,k-2} \;\le\; \tail(k,r) \;\le\; \binom{k}{2}(1-r)^{2}.
\]
\end{proposition}

In the sparse regime ($r$ near $1$) the pair count $\binom{k}{2}$ is therefore sharp to within the factor $r^{k-2}$, and the difficulty parameter is $\asymp nk^{2}(1-r)^{2}$.

\begin{remark}
In the dense regime---$k$ large, $r$ fixed in $(0,1)$---the tail approaches $1$ while $k^{2}(1-r)^{2}$ diverges. Any claim $1-\beta\asymp\min\{1,k^{2}(1-r)^{2}\}$ therefore requires the truncation at $1$; the uniformly valid statement is Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}, in terms of the tail itself.
\end{remark}

\section{\v{C}ech cohomology of the data sheaf: acyclicity}

Fix a field $\bbk$ and take the linear data sheaf $\mathcal{F}(V) = \{f:\text{columns}\to\bbk \mid f|_{V^{c}}=0\}$ with the evident restrictions. For a finite cover $U_{1},\dots,U_{k}$ the ordered \v{C}ech complex is
\[
C^{0} = \bigoplus_{j}\mathcal{F}(U_{j}) \xrightarrow{\ d^{0}\ } C^{1} = \bigoplus_{j<j'}\mathcal{F}(U_{j}\cap U_{j'}) \xrightarrow{\ d^{1}\ }\cdots,\qquad (d^{0}s)_{jj'} = s_{j'} - s_{j}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Degree zero]
The kernel of $d^{0}$ is exactly the image of the map sending a complete record $f$ to $(f|_{U_{j}})_{j}$, which is injective when the $U_{j}$ cover; hence $H^{0}\cong\bbk^{n}$, of dimension the number of columns.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Acyclicity]\label{thm:acyclic}
For every finite cover, $H^{1}=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The data sheaf is flasque: sections extend by zero. Choose for each column $c$ an index $\sigma(c)$ with $c\in U_{\sigma(c)}$; given a cocycle $(t_{jj'})$, set $s_{j}(c) = t_{\sigma(c)\,j}(c)$ for $c\in U_{j}$ and $0$ otherwise. The cocycle relation gives $s_{j'}-s_{j} = t_{jj'}$ on overlaps, so every cocycle is a coboundary.
\end{proof}

Together with Theorem~\ref{thm:glue} this closes the cohomological question for raw records: ``imputation is a sheaf cohomology problem'' is true only in degree $0$, and $H^{1}$ vanishes identically, so no cohomological quantity measures imputation difficulty. A nonvanishing $H^{1}$ demands coefficients with nontrivial restriction maps.

\section{Calibration: where the obstruction lives}

\subsection{The calibration sheaf}
There are $V$ data sources. On the overlap of two sources one observes not records but an additive offset $t_{ab}\in\bbk$---a unit conversion or instrument bias. A \emph{recalibration} is an assignment $s : V\to\bbk$ with
\[
s_{a}-s_{b} = t_{ab} \quad\text{for every recorded overlap } a\text{--}b,
\]
that is, the offsets form a $1$-cochain and realisability is the question of being a coboundary.

\begin{theorem}[Holonomy criterion]\label{thm:holonomy}
Let sources $0,1,2$ overlap pairwise with empty triple overlap. A family $(t_{01},t_{12},t_{20})$ is realisable by per-source recalibrations if and only if
\[
t_{01}+t_{12}+t_{20} = 0.
\]
In particular the family $(1,0,0)$ is \emph{not} realisable: every pairwise comparison is individually consistent, yet no assignment of corrections reconciles them. The corresponding obstruction space has $\dim H^{1} = 1$, over any field and in any characteristic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Necessity: the sum $s_{0}-s_{1}+s_{1}-s_{2}+s_{2}-s_{0}$ telescopes to $0$. Sufficiency: set $s_{0}=0$, $s_{1}=-t_{01}$, $s_{2}=-t_{01}-t_{12}$; the remaining equation is exactly the holonomy condition. The kernel of the coboundary is the constants, so the cokernel is one-dimensional.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The general nerve}
Model the integration problem as a finite multigraph---the \emph{nerve}---with vertex set $V$ and an indexed family $E:\iota\to V\times V$ of oriented overlaps (repeated comparisons allowed). The calibration coboundary is $(ds)_{i} = s_{(Ei)_{1}} - s_{(Ei)_{2}}$.

\begin{theorem}[Nerve Betti theorem]\label{thm:betti}
Let $c$ be the number of connected components of the nerve. Then over any field, in any characteristic,
\[
\dim H^{1} + |V| = |\iota| + c, \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad \dim H^{1} = b_{1}(\text{nerve}) = |\iota| - |V| + c.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$s\in\ker d$ iff $s$ is constant along every overlap, hence constant on every class of the equivalence relation generated by the overlaps; pulling back along the quotient map is an injective linear map from $\bbk^{c}$ onto $\ker d$, so $\dim\ker d = c$ and $\rank d^{0} = |V|-c$. Then $\dim H^{1} = \dim C^{1} - \rank d^{0} - \rank d^{1} = |\iota| - (|V|-c)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Forest criterion]\label{cor:forest}
The calibration problem is solvable for every prescribed family of offsets if and only if the nerve is a forest, i.e.\ $|\iota| + c = |V|$. For a connected nerve, $\dim H^{1} = |\iota| - |V| + 1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}
\emph{Cycle on $m$ sources:} $|\iota| = |V| = m$, $c=1$, so $\dim H^{1} = 1$ for every $m$---independent of the number of sources and of the characteristic; the explicit criterion is again vanishing of the total holonomy. \emph{Star on $m+1$ sources} (a hub compared with every spoke): $|\iota| = m$, $|V| = m+1$, $c=1$, so $\dim H^{1} = 0$; the star is a tree and calibration never obstructs. \emph{Theta graph} (two sources compared through three independent overlaps): $|\iota| = 3$, $|V| = 2$, $c=1$, so $\dim H^{1} = 2$: redundant comparisons, not missing data, create obstructions.
\end{example}

\subsection{Triple overlaps lower the obstruction}
A triple overlap of $a,b,c$ compared through $i:a\text{--}b$, $j:b\text{--}c$, $l:a\text{--}c$ imposes the cocycle relation $t_{i}+t_{j} = t_{l}$: the offset along the diagonal equals the composite along the two sides. Collecting these defines a second coboundary $d^{1}$, and genuine recalibrations satisfy them automatically, so $d^{1}\circ d^{0} = 0$.

\begin{theorem}[Rank formula with triples]\label{thm:triple}
\[
\dim H^{1} + |V| + \rank d^{1} = |\iota| + c.
\]
Hence $\dim H^{1}\le b_{1}(\text{nerve graph})$, with equality iff $\rank d^{1} = 0$: adding triple overlaps can only destroy obstructions, never create them.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Filling a triangle kills the obstruction]
For the three-source cyclic comparison the open triangle has $\dim H^{1} = 1$, while the filled triangle---the same three comparisons together with the triple-overlap relation---has $\dim H^{1} = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Here $|\iota| = |V| = 3$, $c = 1$ and $\rank d^{1} = 1$, so $\dim H^{1} = 3+1-3-1 = 0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:triple}.
\end{proof}

The relevant cohomology is that of the nerve \emph{complex}, not of its $1$-skeleton. Operationally, one record shared by all three sources is worth more than any amount of pairwise reconciliation.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{A. Gluability test and sheaf imputation.} One pass over the table maintaining the first observed value per column; report failure on the first disagreement, otherwise output the completion (observed values where available, a default on wholly unobserved columns) and the completion count $q^{u}$. Complexity $O(nk)$ time, $O(n)$ memory. Completeness is Theorem~\ref{thm:glue}; the count is Theorem~\ref{thm:count}.

\textbf{B. Exact and asymptotic sheaf probability.} Evaluate $\beta = qA^{k}-(q-1)r^{k}$ and return $\beta^{n}$, the tail $1-r^{k}-k(1-r)r^{k-1}$, the sandwich of Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}, and the threshold statistic $n\cdot\tail$. Complexity $O(\log k + \log n)$ with fast exponentiation; work in log-space for large $n$.

\textbf{C. Nerve Betti number.} Compute connected components by union--find and return $|\iota|-|V|+c$; complexity $O((|V|+|\iota|)\alpha(|V|))$. With triple overlaps, compute $\rank d^{1}$ by Gaussian elimination on the relation matrix and subtract.

\textbf{D. Calibration solving with holonomy certificate.} Build a spanning forest, propagate offsets from a root in each component, then test every non-tree overlap; a violated test yields the fundamental cycle of that overlap as an explicit certificate of non-realisability. Complexity $O(|V|+|\iota|)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:betti} the number of independent certificates is exactly $b_{1}$, and by Corollary~\ref{cor:forest} there are none precisely when the nerve is a forest.

\section{Discussion}

\emph{Do not expect sheaf imputation to beat mean imputation on a flat table.} Theorem~\ref{thm:meanimp} makes this precise: on gluable real-valued data they coincide identically. The value of the sheaf viewpoint on a single table lies in Theorem~\ref{thm:count}, which locates all remaining uncertainty at the wholly unobserved columns, and in the $O(nk)$ certificate of Theorem~\ref{thm:glue}.

\emph{Design your comparison graph as a tree.} Corollary~\ref{cor:forest} is actionable: if the graph of cross-validated source pairs is a forest, any pattern of observed offsets can be explained by per-source recalibrations. Every cycle is a potential unfixable inconsistency, and the theta example shows redundant comparison of a single pair produces two independent obstructions. Reference-standard (star) architectures have $b_{1}=0$ by construction.

\emph{When cycles are unavoidable, acquire triple overlaps.} Theorem~\ref{thm:triple} shows they can only reduce the obstruction, and filling a single triangle can annihilate it.

\emph{The right sample-size heuristic} is $n\cdot\Pr[\Bin(k,1-r)\ge2]$---not the missing rate, not the feature count alone. In the sparse regime this is $\asymp nk^{2}(1-r)^{2}$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:reconcile}); in the dense regime it saturates and the tail must be used directly.

\emph{Beware the sign.} More missing data makes the sheaf condition \emph{easier} (Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}). A diagnostic that treats consistency as a proxy for quality is measuring the wrong thing: a table that glues trivially may simply be empty.

\emph{Limitations.} The exact law assumes cellwise-independent MCAR masking with independent uniform values; correlated columns or informative missingness change the per-column factor, though the factorisation over columns survives whenever columns are independent. Acyclicity concerns the constant (flasque) data sheaf. The nerve results assume additive offsets; nonabelian calibration groups give a pointed set rather than a vector space.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Nonabelian calibration.} Replace additive offsets by an arbitrary transformation group; the obstruction becomes a nonabelian $H^{1}$, and one wants the analogue of the forest criterion.
\item \textbf{Higher nerves.} Extend Theorem~\ref{thm:triple} to the full simplicial nerve, with $H^{2}$ and beyond measuring failures among quadruples of sources.
\item \textbf{Non-constant data sheaves.} Identify sheaves---e.g.\ of affine constraints among features---for which sheaf imputation provably improves on mean imputation, with a quantitative rate. Theorem~\ref{thm:meanimp} shows exactly where such a theorem cannot live.
\item \textbf{Correlated missingness.} Extend the exact law to masks with column correlations or missing-not-at-random mechanisms.
\item \textbf{Sharp threshold constant.} Corollary~\ref{cor:threshold} pins the transition to $n\cdot\tail\asymp1$ with a gap factor $1-1/q$; is there a critical constant $\kappa(q)$?
\item \textbf{Obstruction-aware pipeline design.} Given a budget of pairwise comparisons and triple-overlap acquisitions, minimise the expected obstruction dimension---a matroid problem on the cycle space of the nerve.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

Reading a database as a sheaf clarifies, but in the opposite direction to the one expected. For a single table, the apparatus proves there is nothing to gain: gluing is pairwise consistency, completions are counted by the blank columns, mean imputation already returns the sheaf value, and the cohomology vanishes for every cover. Quantitatively, the sheaf condition of a random table holds with probability exactly $(qA^{k}-(q-1)r^{k})^{n}$---exponential in the number of features and increasing in the missing rate---with difficulty governed by $n\cdot\Pr[\Bin(k,1-r)\ge2]$, squeezed between two bounds a factor $1-1/q$ apart. Topologically, the obstruction to reconciling many sources is the first Betti number of the graph of comparisons, reduced by the rank of the triple-overlap relations. Cycles in the integration pipeline, not holes in the table, are what cannot be repaired.

